R-EF1 R-EF3 R-CM7 R-PH12 R-UM1 R-CC8 R-OUT4 R-J3 R-SO3 R-SO7 R-H4 R-EV5 (slice 032b: E1 to E6): the evidence of `tln why` with the fake Fx. 12 runs on 5 projects (all in one dir with a `.git`). The fake `roc version` gives nightly-2026-10-14-38a3029 (`nt`). `compat.tsv` has 4 rows (the 4th is a fail of the platform pfx 0.1.1 on `nt` with no module): json 2.1.0 fails on `nt` in Yaml.roc, json 2.0.0 passes on `nt` and on nightly-2026-10-09-258ab27 (`ot`). The package headers of json 2.1.0 and parser 1.0.0 have the pin `roc: nt`. The calls of one run after the walk, in order (E1 to E3): the compiler (no call with a pin or ROC_TOOLCHAIN, else `path_roc` and the lists), one `read_files` of the `main.roc` of the shown package (rank 3), one `db_query` with the one key (hash, compiler, `any`), and, only for a fail row, the scan of the used modules (one `read_files` for each level, the member text is not read again).
Run 1 (pa, pin nt, imports `json.Wire`): rank 1 is a fail in Yaml.roc and rank 3 is a pass. The fact wins and has the `*` (R-EF3), and the app does not import Yaml, so the note line follows (E4, E5). Run 2: the same with `--json`: `compiler`, `evidence` (a list of `{ rank, source, result, modules, winner }`) and `notes` (E6, R-OUT4). Run 3 (pb imports `json.Yaml`): the fail is used, so there is no note (the scan reads Yaml.roc and then Wire.roc).
Run 4 (pc, no pin, json 2.0.0): the compiler is the `roc` on PATH (rank 7: `path_roc`, the cache, `roc version`): `* 1 local: pass`, no pin in the header of json 2.0.0, so no rank 3 line. Run 5 (the same project, `ROC_TOOLCHAIN` is ot, rank 2): the compiler is ot, and the row of ot is shown: no `path_roc` call. Run 6 (pa with `ROC_TOOLCHAIN` ot): the header pin wins over `ROC_TOOLCHAIN` (the answer of the user for R-SO20): the compiler is nt, as in run 1.
Run 7 (pd, pin ot, parser 1.0.0, `--json`): the compiler is ot. No row for parser and ot, and the pin of the header of parser is nt, which is not ot: no source has a value. `compiler` is ot and `evidence` is `[]`, and the text of the same run would have no block. Run 8 (pe, a path entry, `--json`): a path file has no hash, so no read, no query and no block (R-H4). `compiler` is nt and `evidence` is `[]`. Run 9 (pa, parser): no row, the pin of the header is nt: `* 3 pin: pass` (a hint wins when it is the only source). Run 10 (pa, json) with the 9th db call busy (`fx/db`): the warning of R-CC8 on stderr and no local row (E2): `* 3 pin: pass`, and no note (no fail row, so no scan). Run 11 (pa, json) with the 10th db call too new (`version 2`, R-TF3): `why` stops with exit 19 and prints nothing on stdout. Run 12 (pa, pf): a fail row with no module is shown as `fail` and there is no note, and no scan (a fail with no module is a hard fail, `Units.judge`: nothing for the scan to decide).
